 "observations": [
  "The page number «117» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 6», in bold sans serif, underlined. The titles, in bold, the sigla in roman: L01 that of O, L02 that of C; L03, «Anni collecti (C)» over the left half of the table and «Anni plani (C)» over the right half, C's heads of the two halves. The folios of the manuscripts, «C 46v, O 103r.», stand at the right of L03, the letters v and r raised; here as a margin note. The page has no signature.",
  "The table: a thick and a thin rule above the head, a double rule below it; vertical rules divide the eighteen columns, double ones after the years and before and after the column of the single years; thin rules under the heads that span the four columns of signs, degrees, minutes and seconds, whose heads «Sig.», «Gr.», «Min.», «Sec.» are set in small bold type. The head of the first column, «Anni arabi-ci1) collecti», is printed turned through a quarter, reading upwards, in two lines; here it stands in the first row (L04). Each printed row is one line here, the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The anomaly of the collected years ends with 570 (L26); the rows 600-720 (L27-L31) have the moon only.",
  "L07-L31, the collected years. Checked against al-Khwārizmī's parameters, Brahmagupta's (p. 42, JP2 0074): 57 753 300 000 revolutions of the moon and 488 105 858 of its apogee in 1 577 916 450 000 days, a daily motion of the moon of 13° 10′ 34″ 52‴ and of its anomaly of 13° 3′ 53″ 58‴. Thirty Arab years, 10 631 days, move the moon by 1s 7° 50′ 4.9″ and its anomaly by 9s 23° 57′ 27.2″. From the radices 3s 27° 45′ 17″ (117° 45′ 17″) and 3s 14° 36′ 1″ the rows agree with the multiples of these steps to within 3.7″ and 3.9″, the seconds growing by 4 or 5 and by 26 or 27 (with the readings of notes b-e and the editor's 7 of note f, where C and O have 50). The row 690 has the sign 9 where the step requires 8: between 7s 20° 7′ 2″ (660) and 10s 5° 47′ 10″ (720) it is 8s 27° 57′ 6″ (computed 8s 27° 57′ 8.8″); the rows 600-720 are those that O's second hand added (note a-a); no note bears on the 9.",
  "L07-L36, the single years. Checked: the year p moves the moon and the anomaly by the days of the first p Arab years, 354 days and 355 in the leap years 2, 5, 7, 10, 13, 16, 18, 21, 24, 26 and 29 (Tab. 2, p. 110, JP2 0142): all 30 rows of the anomaly agree to within 3.7″, and 29 rows of the moon to within 1.5″ (with the readings of notes g-q, s-v and x-z; C's 16 and 14 of notes o and q are nearer the computation, 16.2″ and 13.9″, than O's 15 and 13, which the edition prints). The year 30, 1s 7° 50′ 4″ and 9s 23° 57′ 27″, is the step of the collected years.",
  "L32 and N07, the year 26 of the moon: the edition prints 2s 26° 56′ 44″ against the 20″ of C and O (note r). The computation gives 2s 26° 56′ 20.5″: the year 26 is a leap year, and its 355 days move the moon on from the year 25, 2s 29° 19′ 57″, by 11s 27° 36′ 22″, to 2s 26° 56′ 19″-20″; the seconds 44 are those of a common year of 354 days (57″ less 13″), while the degrees and minutes 26° 56′ are those of the leap year. The year 27, 354 days later, 2s 11° 22′ 7″, follows from 20″ (20″ less 13″), not from 44″. The manuscripts' reading is right and the edition's emendation is wrong; the table is transcribed as printed.",
  "N09-N11: Ms. C's note in the lower margin: multiply the days of the moon by 13 and add the product to the place in which the moon overtook the sun, and you find the place of the moon in signs and degrees; subtract the days of the moon from the days of the sun; «felek elthedewir», the sphere of the epicycle (falak al-tadwīr). The ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. Italics: «om.», «addid.», «man. sec.», «corr. ex», «Addid.», «in marg. infer.:», the colon set close.",
  "Several long dashes of the apparatus are printed broken in two pieces with a narrow gap (N03, after «elheza C»; N05, after «50 CO»; N06, after «1 C»; N07, at the beginning of the line and after «14 C») or shorter than the others (N05, after «27 O» and at the end of the line); read as long dashes. A speck before the «4» of the single years (L10) and specks in the margins; not transcribed."
 ],
